Guided Exercise: Find and Inspect Container Images
Use a supported MySQL container image to run server and client pods in Kubernetes; also test two community images and compare their runtime requirements.
Outcomes
Locate and run container images from a container registry.
Inspect remote container images and container logs.
Set environment variables and override entry points for a container.
Access files and directories within a container.

Find and Inspect Container Images
1. Start the Lab
Prepare the environment.
lab start pods-images
This command:
•	resets the environment
•	prepares the OpenShift cluster
•	creates required resources.
________________________________________
2. Login to the OpenShift Cluster
Use the oc CLI of Red Hat OpenShift.
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Login as:
username: developer
password: developer
________________________________________
3. Create a Project (Namespace)
Projects isolate resources.
oc new-project pods-images
Now all pods will run inside:
pods-images
________________________________________
4. Login to the Container Registry
Use Skopeo to authenticate.
skopeo login registry.ocp4.example.com:8443
Enter:
Username: developer
Password: developer
________________________________________
5. List Available Image Tags
Check available versions of nginx.
skopeo list-tags docker://registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/docker-nginx
Example output:
1.23
1.23-alpine
1.23-alpine-perl
1.23-perl
latest
These are image tags (versions).
________________________________________
6. Create nginx Pod
oc run docker-nginx \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/docker-nginx:1.23
Check pod status:
oc get pods
Output:
docker-nginx   CrashLoopBackOff
Meaning:
pod repeatedly fails
________________________________________
7. Check Pod Logs
Logs help identify the problem.
oc logs docker-nginx
Error example:
mkdir() "/var/cache/nginx/client_temp" failed (13: Permission denied)
Reason:
nginx needs root permissions
________________________________________
8. Debug the Pod
Create debug container.
oc debug pod/docker-nginx
Check directory permissions.
ls -la /etc | grep nginx
ls -la /var/cache | grep nginx
Result:
owner = root
Only root can write.
________________________________________
9. Check Running User
whoami
Example:
1000770000
Meaning:
OpenShift assigned random non-root UID
________________________________________
10. Inspect Image Configuration
Use OpenShift command.
oc image info registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/docker-nginx:1.23
Important section:
Entrypoint: /docker-entrypoint.sh
Command: nginx -g daemon off;
But:
USER field missing
Meaning:
container must run as root
OpenShift blocks this for security.
________________________________________
11. Delete Failed Pod
oc delete pod docker-nginx
________________________________________
12. Inspect Bitnami MySQL Image
List tags.
skopeo list-tags docker://registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/bitnami-mysql
Tags:
8.0.28
8.0.29
8.0.30
8.0.31
latest
________________________________________
13. Inspect Image Metadata
oc image info registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/bitnami-mysql:8.0.31
Important:
User: 1001
Meaning:
non-root container
So OpenShift allows it.
________________________________________
14. Create MySQL Pod
oc run bitnami-mysql \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/bitnami-mysql:8.0.31
Check:
oc get pods
Result:
CrashLoopBackOff
________________________________________
15. Check Logs
oc logs bitnami-mysql
Error:
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD environment variable is empty
Meaning:
MySQL requires root password
________________________________________
16. Recreate Pod With Environment Variable
Delete:
oc delete pod bitnami-mysql
Create again:
oc run bitnami-mysql \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/bitnami-mysql:8.0.31 \
--env MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=redhat123
Check:
oc get pods
Now:
Running
________________________________________
17. Check Container UID
Execute command inside pod.
oc exec -it bitnami-mysql -- /bin/bash -c "whoami && id"
Example:
uid=1000770000
OpenShift replaces image UID with namespace UID.
________________________________________
18. Check Project UID Range
oc describe project pods-images
Example:
openshift.io/sa.scc.uid-range=1000770000/10000
Meaning:
pods must run within this UID range
________________________________________
19. Inspect Supported Red Hat MySQL Image
List tags:
skopeo list-tags docker://registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80
Tags include:
1-224
1-228
1-237
latest
1
Tag types
Floating tags:
latest
1
Fixed tags:
1-237
Source tags:
1-224-source
________________________________________
20. Inspect Image Release
skopeo inspect docker://registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:latest
Better formatted output:
skopeo inspect --format \
"Name: {{.Name}}\nDigest: {{.Digest}}\nRelease: {{.Labels.release}}" \
docker://registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:latest
Output:
Release: 237
________________________________________
21. Run Red Hat MySQL Pod
oc run rhel9-mysql \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:1-237
Check:
oc get pods
Result:
CrashLoopBackOff
________________________________________
22. Check Logs
oc logs rhel9-mysql
Error:
MYSQL_USER
MYSQL_PASSWORD
MYSQL_DATABASE
Variables missing.
________________________________________
23. Recreate Pod With Variables
Delete:
oc delete pod rhel9-mysql
Create again:
oc run rhel9-mysql \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:1-237 \
--env MYSQL_USER=redhat \
--env MYSQL_PASSWORD=redhat123 \
--env MYSQL_DATABASE=worldx
Now:
Running
________________________________________
24. Inspect MySQL Data Directory
Inspect image:
oc image info registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:1-237
Important variable:
HOME=/var/lib/mysql
Check inside container:
oc exec -it rhel9-mysql -- ls -la /var/lib/mysql
Database directory:
/var/lib/mysql/data
Check database:
oc exec -it rhel9-mysql -- ls /var/lib/mysql/data
Result:
worldx
________________________________________
25. Get Pod IP
MYSQL_IP=$(oc get pods rhel9-mysql -o=jsonpath='{.status.podIP}')
Example:
10.8.0.69
________________________________________
26. Create MySQL Client Pod
oc run mysqlclient \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/rhel9/mysql-80:1-237 \
--env MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=redhat123
________________________________________
27. Connect to MySQL Server
Run MySQL client:
oc exec -it mysqlclient \
-- mysqlshow -u redhat -p -h $MYSQL_IP
Password:
redhat123
Output:
Databases
information_schema
performance_schema
worldx
Connection successful.
________________________________________
28. Finish Lab
Clean environment.
lab finish pods-images
________________________________________
Final Concept Summary
Tools Used
Tool	Purpose
oc	manage OpenShift cluster
skopeo	inspect container images
oc logs	check container logs
oc exec	run commands in container
________________________________________
Important Debug Steps
When pod fails:
1️⃣ Check status
oc get pods
2️⃣ Check logs
oc logs POD
3️⃣ Inspect image
oc image info IMAGE
4️⃣ Debug container
oc debug pod/POD
________________________________________
Key Reasons Pods Fail
Common causes:
•	missing environment variables
•	permission issues
•	container needs root
•	wrong configuration

